1. Health Checks & Probes:
    - In Kubernetes, health checks (Probes) are ways for the system to continuously check whether your application inside a container is working properly or not.
    - Think of Kubernetes like a manager running your app. It doesn’t just start your app and forget it—it keeps asking:
        “Are you alive?”
        “Are you ready to serve users?”
        “Should I restart you?”
    - That’s exactly what probes do.
    
    - Types of Kubernetes Probes
    1. Liveness Probe (Is the app alive?)
        This checks: “Is the container stuck or crashed internally?”
        If it fails: Kubernetes restarts the container
        Example idea: Your Node.js app is running but stuck in an infinite loop → liveness probe detects it and restarts it.
        Use case:
            Deadlocks
            Memory leaks
            Frozen apps
    2. Readiness Probe (Is the app ready to receive traffic?)
        This checks: “Can this app accept user requests right now?”
        If it fails:    
            Kubernetes removes the pod from Service traffic
            BUT it does NOT restart the container
        Example idea: Your app is running but still loading database connections → not ready yet.
        Use case:
            Startup delay
            Dependency loading (DB, APIs)
            Heavy initialization tasks
    3. Startup Probe (Is the app finished starting?)
        This is for slow-starting apps.
        It checks: “Has the app fully started yet?”
        If startup probe is enabled:
            Liveness & readiness are paused until startup succeeds
            Prevents premature restarts
        Use case:
            Java Spring Boot apps
            Large ML models
            Heavy initialization containers
    
    - How probes work (simple flow)
        Container starts
        Startup probe runs (optional)
        Liveness probe keeps checking: “still alive?”
        Readiness probe checks: “send traffic or not?”
    - Example YAML (simple)
        livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
                path: /health
                port: 3000
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 10
        readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
                path: /ready
                port: 3000
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 5

    - Real-world analogy
        Imagine a restaurant:
            Liveness probe → Is the chef unconscious? (restart chef 😄)
            Readiness probe → Is food actually ready to serve? (don’t send customers yet)
            Startup probe → Is the kitchen even open yet?
    
    - Why probes are important
        Without probes:
            Kubernetes may send traffic to broken apps
            Or keep dead apps running forever
        With probes:
            Better reliability
            Auto-healing systems
            Zero-downtime deployments

